  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Susie pays for \\(4\\) muffins and \\(3\\) bananas. Calvin spends twice as much paying for \\(2\\) muffins and \\(16\\) bananas. A muffin is how many times as expensive as a banana?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{13}{4}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": "5/3"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2014-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let a muffin cost \\(m\\) and a banana cost \\(b.\\) Then \\[ 2(4m+3b) = 2m+16b. \\]\n\nExpanding gives \\(8m+6b = 2m+16b,\\) so \\(6m = 10b\\) and \\(m = \\tfrac53 b.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Let a muffin cost \\(m\\) and a banana cost \\(b\\)",
        "Calvin spending twice as much means \\(2(4m+3b)=2m+16b\\)"
      ],
